Title: Night Shift
Credit: Written by
Author: Dana Whitlock
Draft date: October 28, 2026
Contact:
    Whitlock Pictures
    Route 9, Box 14

FADE IN:

INT. ALL-NIGHT DINER - 3:12 A.M.

Rain streaks the windows. A neon sign buzzes OPEN, though the P gave up years ago.

MARGO HALE (50s), a night dispatcher in a cardigan two sizes too big, works the crossword in pen.

The bell over the door rings. THEO (20s), soaked through, clutches a shoebox like it might hatch.

THEO
Are you the one who answers the radio?

MARGO
(not looking up)
Depends who's calling.

THEO
Nobody. That's the problem. It's been calling all week.

He sets the shoebox on the counter. Inside: a transistor radio, switched off, hissing anyway.

Margo puts down her pen.

CUT TO:

EXT. RADIO TOWER - CONTINUOUS

A red light blinks at the top of the mast. Blinks. Then holds steady, like it's listening.

INT. MARGO'S CAR - MOMENTS LATER

Wipers on high. Theo rides shotgun with the shoebox on his knees.

MARGO
How'd you find me?

THEO
The radio gave me the address.

MARGO
(beat)
That's not a thing radios do.

THEO
I know. I wrote it down anyway.

He holds up a napkin. Typed on it, in faded ribbon ink: her name, her street, tonight's date.

EXT. COUNTY ROAD - NIGHT

Headlights carve through the fog. A mile marker slides past: 0.

INT. DISPATCH OFFICE - LATER

A basement room. One lamp. A wall of reel-to-reel decks, every one of them turning.

Margo stops in the doorway.

MARGO
I unplugged these in 1994.
